 give, and <strong>149 record highs</strong>, more than the 114 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (63). Portland's all-time high is 116\u00b0F, on June 28, 2021.","t_chg":"Portland has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 85 complete years: Portland's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.46\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.37 to +0.55), about +3.9\u00b0F since 1941. Portland's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.36\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.24 to +0.47), about +3.0\u00b0F since 1941.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 10 of 12 months. Highs rose in 8 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Portland's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.18 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 36.9 inches a year. The wettest year was 1996 (63.20 in); the driest, 1985 (22.48 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}